### Description
The Terraform program below creates an AWS Route 53 traffic policy. The traffic policy is defined using the `aws_route53_traffic_policy` resource, which requires a `document` attribute specifying the policy's configuration and a `name` attribute for the policy's name. The `document` attribute should be properly formatted according to AWS Route 53 traffic policy specifications.

### Terraform HCL Program

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

resource "aws_route53_traffic_policy" "my_route53_traffic_policy" {
  name    = "example-policy"
  comment = "Example Route 53 Traffic Policy"

  document = jsonencode({
    CallerReference = "1234567890"
    Version         = "2015-10-01"
    TrafficPolicy   = {
      Name     = "example-policy"
      Comment  = "Example Route 53 Traffic Policy"
      Document = {
        AWSPolicyFormatVersion = "2015-10-01"
        RecordType             = "A"
        StartEndpoint          = "MyFailoverEndpoint"
        Endpoints              = {
          MyFailoverEndpoint = {
            Type = "FAILOVER"
            Failover = {
              Primary = {
                EndpointReference = "PrimaryDNS"
              }
              Secondary = {
                EndpointReference = "SecondaryDNS"
              }
            }
          }
          PrimaryDNS = {
            Type = "VALUE"
            Value = {
              TTL   = 300
              Record = {
                Type   = "A"
                Values = ["192.0.2.1"]
              }
            }
          }
          SecondaryDNS = {
            Type = "VALUE"
            Value = {
              TTL   = 300
              Record = {
                Type   = "A"
                Values = ["192.0.2.2"]
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  })
}
```

This Terraform configuration sets up a basic failover traffic policy with two endpoints, `PrimaryDNS` and `SecondaryDNS`. The primary endpoint points to `192.0.2.1`, and the secondary endpoint points to `192.0.2.2`. The policy is configured to route traffic to the primary endpoint unless it fails, in which case it routes traffic to the secondary endpoint.

