### Description

The provided Terraform configuration creates a DynamoDB table named "ExampleTable" with a hash key "Id" and an additional attribute "Name". It also inserts an item into this table with the hash key "1" and the name "ExampleItem". The configuration ensures that the DynamoDB table item resource depends on the existence of the DynamoDB table.

### Terraform HCL Program

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-2"
}

resource "aws_dynamodb_table" "example_dynamodb_table" {
  name           = "ExampleTable"
  billing_mode   = "PAY_PER_REQUEST"
  hash_key       = "Id"

  attribute {
    name = "Id"
    type = "S"
  }

  attribute {
    name = "Name"
    type = "S"
  }

  # Ensure that the "Name" attribute is indexed
  global_secondary_index {
    name               = "NameIndex"
    hash_key           = "Name"
    projection_type    = "ALL"
  }
}

resource "aws_dynamodb_table_item" "example_dynamodb_table_item" {
  table_name = aws_dynamodb_table.example_dynamodb_table.name
  hash_key   = aws_dynamodb_table.example_dynamodb_table.hash_key
  item       = jsonencode({
    Id   = {"S": "1"}
    Name = {"S": "ExampleItem"}
  })
}
```

This configuration includes a global secondary index on the "Name" attribute to ensure that it is indexed, which resolves the error reported by Terraform.

